course: politics
lesson: 19
title: Immigration and borders
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Think of the new kid who joins your class in the middle of the year. Maybe her dad got a job as a doctor at the hospital, maybe her family ran from a war. People move to another country for many reasons: work, study, family or danger.
explain: A country is a bit like a school with a front gate: it decides who may come in and how. People argue hard about it: how many should come, who, by which routes, and what should happen to people who came without permission.
explain: Most people on all sides say they want fairness, safety and fewer people in danger; they disagree about the way to get there. Even the words are part of the argument, so listen to which words each side chooses.
explain: Big claims like "all of them" or "millions flooding in" are worth checking against official numbers; the numbers can be big without matching what a scary video says.
explain: People talk about it in their own words: a South African says "just now" for "later" and "lekker" for great, an Indian English speaker may say "prepone" (to move earlier, the opposite of postpone), an Aussie calls a petrol station a "servo", and an American teen says "no cap" for "no lie".
fact: People move to another country for jobs, study, family or to escape danger; an immigrant moves in, someone who emigrates moves away, and a visa is official permission to come and stay.
fact: Many countries invite workers they need, like doctors, nurses and builders; some say numbers have been too high for housing and services, others say countries need these workers.
fact: A refugee has fled war or danger, and asylum is protection given by a country; after Russia's full-scale invasion in 2022, millions of Ukrainians fled abroad, and countries like the UK, Ireland and Canada set up special schemes.
fact: Some people enter or stay without permission; some call this "illegal immigration", saying the word is accurate and respects the law, while others say "undocumented" or "irregular", saying it describes the paperwork, not the person.
fact: It was reported that many people tried to cross the US border with Mexico without permission around 2022 and 2023, and that crossings dropped sharply in 2024 and 2025 after rule changes; some parts have fences or walls, others none.
fact: News reports said that in 2025 the US government greatly increased deportations, sending people found living there without permission back to their home countries; there were protests and court cases about how it was done.
fact: It was reported that tens of thousands of people a year have crossed the English Channel from France in small boats to ask for asylum in the UK; the crossings are dangerous, smugglers charge money for them, and some people have died.
fact: An asylum seeker has asked for protection and is waiting for an answer; it was reported that the UK had a big backlog of claims, a pile of cases waiting to be dealt with, many people were housed in hotels paid by the government, and most are not allowed to work while waiting.
fact: It was reported that Australia has for many years turned back boats and sent people arriving by boat to centres in other countries, called offshore processing, while also resettling some refugees each year.
fact: Immigrants both take jobs and create them by working, starting businesses and spending money; experts disagree about the exact effects, which differ by place and type of work.
fact: After living legally in a country for some years and passing tests, immigrants can become citizens with the right to vote; many countries hold citizenship ceremonies with an oath.
fact: Net migration is the number of people who arrived minus the number who left; official statistics try to count it, and figures get updated.
fact: Some say strict rules and strong borders keep a country safe and fair to those who come legally; others say countries should offer more safe, legal routes and treat everyone with dignity.
exercise: Why might a doctor move to another country? | For a job, because many countries invite workers they need | Because every doctor must move to a new country each year | Because a law says all doctors must work abroad once | To avoid going to work, since doctors abroad do not have to work
exercise: What is a visa? | Official permission to come to and stay in a country | A special photo that goes inside your passport | A bank card that travellers use to pay abroad | A ticket that lets you board any plane anywhere in the world
exercise: What is a refugee? | A person who has fled war or danger in their own country | A tourist who is having a long holiday somewhere sunny | A person who has never once left the town they were born in | A guard who checks passports at the border
exercise: What is asylum? | Protection given by a country to someone fleeing danger | A cheap hotel where travellers stay for one night | A tax that every traveller pays at the airport | A tall wall built along a country's border
exercise: What did countries like the UK, Ireland and Canada do after 2022, as reported? | Set up special schemes to take in Ukrainians fleeing the war | Closed all their borders to Ukrainians | Sent soldiers to guard every school in their country | Stopped giving visas to anyone from anywhere, forever
exercise: Mateo hears "illegal immigrants" and "undocumented migrants". What is true? | They mostly mean the same people; the word shows a view | They are two completely different groups who never meet | One means tourists and the other means workers with jobs, so they never overlap | Both words mean refugees and nobody else at all
exercise: What was reported about crossings at the US–Mexico border in 2024 and 2025? | They dropped sharply after rule changes | They rose to the highest number ever recorded, every single month | Nobody counted them, so no numbers exist | The border was removed and anyone could cross freely
exercise: What is deportation? | When a government sends someone back to their home country | When a family goes abroad on a summer holiday by plane or ferry | When a citizen goes to vote in an election | When a person is given a visa to stay
exercise: What is the English Channel? | The strip of sea between England and France | A TV channel that shows programmes about England | A long river that runs right through London | A tunnel under the sea used by cars only
exercise: What do smugglers do? | Charge money to move people across borders secretly | Rescue people at sea for free and bring them safely to land | Check passports at airports and ports | Build safe and legal routes for travellers
exercise: What is an asylum seeker? | Someone who has asked for protection and is waiting for an answer | Someone who has already become a full citizen | A border officer who decides who gets asylum | A tourist who came with a visa for a short holiday and stayed on
exercise: What is a backlog? | A pile of cases waiting to be dealt with | A log fire burning in the back garden at night | A list of all the new laws passed this year | A small wooden boat used to cross the sea
exercise: What is offshore processing? | Dealing with asylum claims in another country | Fishing for a living far out at sea | Checking passports on a plane before it lands | Building new homes along the beach
exercise: Do immigrants only take jobs? | No, they also create jobs by working, starting businesses and spending | Yes, because they never start businesses of their own | Yes, because they send all their money away and never spend it here | No, because they are never allowed to work at all
exercise: What does a citizenship ceremony mark? | An immigrant becoming a full citizen with the right to vote | A tourist arriving at the airport and having a passport stamped | A refugee being sent back home | A visa running out and ending
exercise: What is net migration? | The number who arrived minus the number who left | The number of fishing nets used out at sea each year | Only the number of people who arrived in a year | Only the number of people who left in a year
exercise: Where can you find real immigration numbers? | Official statistics from government agencies | The caption under a video that went viral | A rumour that everyone heard in the playground | A meme page that posts lots of shocking numbers
test: A new girl from Pakistan joins the class; her dad is a doctor. Someone asks, "Why'd she even come here?" What is the best answer? | People move for many reasons, like jobs, and a new classmate is a person first | She must be here on holiday and will go home soon | Only refugees ever move to another country | Doctors are made to move to other countries by law, so she had no choice
test: «is the new boy from Ukraine a refugee or nah» What is the answer? | Yes, if his family fled the war for safety | No, because refugees are really just tourists | No, because there is no war in Ukraine | Yes, because every new pupil in any school is a refugee
test: Mateo's brother asks at the border fence, "Is that to keep people out or in?" What do people argue about? | Whether walls fix the reasons people try to cross | Whether the fence is made of steel or of wood | Whether Mexico is really a country or not | Whether the deserts near the border get hot in summer
test: "If he broke the rules, he has to go" says a boy, while a girl cries about her uncle. Why is this so hard to argue about? | A rule and a family can both be real | The boy is always wrong because rules do not matter | The girl is always wrong because crying is not an argument | Rules never matter when somebody is sad
test: Ethan's gran in Dover says "It's dangerous"; his grandad says "It has to stop". What do most people agree on? | Dangerous crossings and the smugglers who profit should be stopped | The crossings are quite safe, so there is nothing at all to worry about | Nobody really uses small boats any more | Smugglers help people for free and do not charge money
test: «the hotels cost a fortune, why don't they just get a job lol» What did the charity worker point out? | Most asylum seekers are not allowed to work while they wait | They all have full-time jobs already and go to them every day | Hotels are free, so they cost the country nothing at all | They are allowed to work but refuse to
test: Grace's aunt says turning back boats saved lives; her uncle says it was too harsh. What is fair? | The same policy can be seen as protection or harshness depending on what you look at | Only the aunt has a real view, because the uncle is just upset | Australia has no policy about boats at all | Boats never reach Australia, so the argument is pointless
test: Dylan: "Immigrants take all our jobs." Aisha: "My parents made jobs!" What is wrong with Dylan's claim? | The word "all" is almost never true; immigrants take and create jobs | Nothing is wrong, because immigrants never start businesses or hire anyone | Aisha's parents cannot own a shop | Jobs cannot be made, only taken
test: "Lekker, she's Canadian now!" says Fatima's South African neighbour (lekker = great) after the oath and cake. What can Fatima's mum now do? | Vote as a full citizen | Only visit Canada as a tourist from now on | Never leave Canada again for the rest of her life | Only work in one job that the government picks
test: «video says millions flooding in no cap» (no cap = no lie). Liam's friend shows a government page. What should Liam do? | Check the official number before sharing the panic | Trust the video, because it is loud and lots of people watched it | Share both and pick the scarier one | Ignore every number from everyone
test: What would happen if a country decided asylum claims much faster? | People would wait less time in hotels for an answer | Every single claim would be refused straight away, without a hearing | Nobody would ever ask for asylum again | The Channel would close to boats
test: A TV host says "irregular migration" and another says "illegal immigration". What can you learn from the words? | Each word choice tells you something about the speaker's view | Only one of them is speaking proper English | They are both talking about tourists on holiday | The words mean refugees only and nobody else, so they say exactly the same
exam: Identify the term which denotes a person who has fled war or persecution in the country of his or her nationality. | A refugee | A tourist travelling abroad | A citizen by birth | A border officer at a port
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "net migration"? | The number of persons arriving, less the number of persons departing, within a given period | The total number of persons arriving within a given period, without regard to those departing | The number of persons granted citizenship in a given year | The number of vessels crossing a stretch of sea
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning persons awaiting a decision upon an asylum claim in the United Kingdom, as reported. | Most such persons are not permitted to undertake employment while awaiting a decision | All such persons are required to undertake full-time employment from the day of their arrival | Such persons are granted citizenship immediately upon arrival | Such persons receive a decision on the day of arrival
exam: Whereas one speaker employs the term "illegal immigration" and another the term "irregular migration", determine the principal significance of the difference. | The choice of term reflects the speaker's view, although both refer largely to the same persons | The terms denote wholly unrelated groups of persons, who are never to be confused with one another | One term refers exclusively to tourists | Neither term refers to any person at all
